\]

Given that the distance between any two distinct points in \(S\) is \(d\), we can infer that if we choose \(y\) appropriately, the above conditions will hold. One way to achieve this is to choose \(y\) as the centroid (mean) of the points in \(S\). However, in infinite dimensions, finding a precise centroid might not be straightforward. Instead, we can consider a more geometric approach.

By the properties of Hilbert spaces and the fact that the distances are constant, we can choose \(y\) such that the set of vectors \(\left\{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{d}(x - y) : x \in S\right\}\) forms an orthonormal basis for \(\mathcal{H}\).

Thus, the point \(y\) that satisfies these conditions is the centroid of the set \(S\), but the exact coordinates of \(y\) may need further analysis depending on the specific configuration of \(S\).

In conclusion, we have shown that there exists a point \(y \in \mathcal{H}\) such that the set 

